<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hecataeus of Miletus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 550–c. 476 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hecataeus of Miletus was an early Greek historian and geographer. His Periodos Ges, a description of the known world arranged as a coastal voyage, and his Genealogies, which treated myths critically, survive only in fragments quoted by later writers. He took part in the deliberations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> and was a major source and model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>.</p></div>
